// CORE 2 — 220-1202 questions
// Domain 1.0 Operating Systems
{id:"c2-1.1-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.1",objectiveText:"Identify basic features of Microsoft Windows editions.",question:"A home user needs a new PC for personal use — web browsing, streaming, and gaming. They do not need BitLocker, domain join, or Group Policy management. Which Windows 11 edition is appropriate?",options:["Windows 11 Pro","Windows 11 Enterprise","Windows 11 Home","Windows 11 Education"],correct:2,explanation:"Windows 11 Home is the consumer edition for personal use. It includes core Windows features (Windows Hello, Windows Defender, Microsoft Store, WSL) but lacks enterprise-only features such as BitLocker full-drive encryption, Active Directory domain join, Group Policy management, Remote Desktop host capability, and Hyper-V. It is the appropriate and least expensive choice for home users.",wrongReasons:["Windows 11 Pro adds BitLocker, domain join, Group Policy, Remote Desktop host, and Hyper-V on top of Home; it is designed for business and power users, not required for basic home use.","Windows 11 Enterprise is the highest-tier edition adding advanced security (Credential Guard, Application Guard) and enterprise licensing; it is acquired through volume licensing, not retail purchase.","null","Windows 11 Education mirrors Enterprise features and is licensed through academic volume agreements for schools and institutions."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.1 — Windows Editions",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.1 — Windows Editions",messerSection:"1.1",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-1.1-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.1",objectiveText:"Identify basic features of Microsoft Windows editions.",question:"A technician is checking whether a PC can officially upgrade to Windows 11. The CPU and RAM meet requirements, but one hardware component is missing that Windows 11 strictly requires. Which component does Windows 11 require that Windows 10 did not enforce?",options:["Dedicated GPU with DirectX 12","USB 3.0 controller","TPM 2.0 (Trusted Platform Module version 2.0)","At least 16 GB of RAM"],correct:2,explanation:"Windows 11 requires TPM 2.0 as a hard prerequisite. TPM 2.0 enables secure boot measurement, Windows Hello credential storage, and BitLocker encryption key sealing. Microsoft added this requirement to raise the security baseline. Systems without TPM 2.0 cannot officially upgrade to Windows 11 through normal channels.",wrongReasons:["A dedicated GPU is not required; Windows 11 runs on integrated graphics (Intel UHD, AMD Radeon integrated) as long as it supports DirectX 12.","USB 3.0 is not a Windows 11 hardware requirement; USB 2.0 ports are sufficient.","null","Windows 11 requires a minimum of 4 GB RAM; 16 GB is recommended for performance but not required."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.1 — Windows 11 Requirements",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.1 — Windows Editions",messerSection:"1.1",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-1.1-003",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.1",objectiveText:"Identify basic features of Microsoft Windows editions.",question:"A small business needs laptops that support BitLocker full-drive encryption and Active Directory domain join. Which is the MINIMUM Windows 11 edition supporting both features?",options:["Windows 11 Home","Windows 11 Pro","Windows 11 Enterprise only","Windows 11 SE"],correct:1,explanation:"Windows 11 Pro is the minimum edition that includes both BitLocker Drive Encryption and Active Directory domain join capability. Windows 11 Home lacks both. Pro also adds Group Policy management, Remote Desktop host, Hyper-V, and Windows Sandbox compared to Home. Enterprise is not needed just for these two features.",wrongReasons:["Windows 11 Home does not include BitLocker (full-drive encryption) or the ability to join an Active Directory domain — both require Pro or higher.","null","Enterprise includes all Pro features plus advanced capabilities; it is not the minimum edition since Pro already provides BitLocker and domain join.","Windows 11 SE is a locked-down cloud-first edition designed for lower-end educational devices; it does not support standard enterprise management features."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.1 — Windows Editions",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.1 — Windows Editions",messerSection:"1.1",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-1.1-004",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.1",objectiveText:"Identify basic features of Microsoft Windows editions.",question:"A technician formats a 256 GB USB drive to be used on both Windows and macOS computers, and it must store files larger than 4 GB. Which file system should be used?",options:["FAT32","NTFS","exFAT","ReFS"],correct:2,explanation:"exFAT (Extended FAT) is designed for removable media and supports files larger than 4 GB (FAT32's 4 GB per-file limit). exFAT is natively readable and writable on both Windows and macOS without additional drivers. It is the optimal choice for large USB drives shared across operating systems.",wrongReasons:["FAT32 is broadly compatible but has a maximum individual file size of 4 GB — insufficient for large video files, disk images, or virtual machines.","NTFS is Windows' native file system; macOS can read NTFS drives but cannot write to them natively without third-party software, making it impractical for a cross-platform drive.","null","ReFS (Resilient File System) is a Microsoft server/storage file system for large-scale data integrity; it is not supported by macOS and is not designed for removable USB drives."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.1 — File Systems",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.1 — Windows File Systems",messerSection:"1.1",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-1.2-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.2",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, use the appropriate Microsoft command-line tool.",question:"An enterprise is deploying Windows 11 to 500 new laptops. On first boot, each laptop connects to the network, downloads its configuration and applications from a cloud management system, and configures itself with no technician interaction. Which deployment method is this?",options:["Clean install from bootable USB","Sysprep unattended install only","Zero-touch deployment","In-place upgrade"],correct:2,explanation:"Zero-touch deployment provisions new devices automatically without any manual technician steps. The device boots, contacts the cloud deployment service (such as Microsoft Intune / Windows Autopilot), downloads the configuration, installs required software, and applies policies — the technician ships the device directly to the user. This scales efficiently for large deployments.",wrongReasons:["A clean install from USB requires a technician to physically insert media, boot from it, and step through setup; it is a manual process, not zero-touch.","Sysprep creates a generalized master image but still requires manual steps to capture and deploy the image; by itself it is not zero-touch.","null","An in-place upgrade upgrades the OS while preserving files and apps on an existing system; it is not a method for deploying and configuring new devices at scale."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.2 — OS Installations",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.2 — Installing Operating Systems",messerSection:"1.2",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-1.3-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.3",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, use features and tools of the Microsoft Windows OS.",question:"A technician needs to view all active network connections on a Windows workstation including the protocol, local and remote addresses, connection state, and the Process ID (PID) owning each connection. Which command provides this?",options:["ipconfig /all","ping -t localhost","netstat -ano","tracert 8.8.8.8"],correct:2,explanation:"'netstat -ano' displays all active TCP and UDP connections and listening ports (-a), shows addresses and ports in numeric form without DNS resolution (-n), and includes the owning Process ID for each connection (-o). The PID can then be matched in Task Manager to identify the specific application holding each connection.",wrongReasons:["ipconfig /all shows network adapter configuration (IP address, subnet mask, gateway, DNS, DHCP lease) but does not list active connections or listening ports.","ping -t sends continuous ICMP echo requests to test connectivity to a target; it does not show active connections or port usage.","null","tracert traces the routing path to a destination host; it does not show local connections or which application is using each port."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.3 — Windows Tools",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.3 — Windows Command-Line Tools",messerSection:"1.3",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-1.3-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.3",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, use features and tools of the Microsoft Windows OS.",question:"A user's Windows PC starts slowly and several unknown applications launch at every login. Which built-in Windows tool lets a technician view and disable individual startup programs?",options:["Services.msc","Regedit.exe","Task Manager — Startup tab","msconfig — Boot tab"],correct:2,explanation:"Task Manager's Startup tab (opened with Ctrl+Shift+Esc) lists all programs configured to launch at user logon, shows their startup impact (High/Medium/Low), and lets the technician enable or disable each entry. Since Windows 8, the startup program list was moved from msconfig's Startup tab into Task Manager.",wrongReasons:["Services.msc manages Windows background services; it does not show or manage application startup entries that launch at user logon.","Regedit can manually edit startup registry keys (Run keys in HKLM and HKCU) but is not the recommended tool for non-expert startup management.","null","msconfig's Boot tab controls boot options such as Safe Mode and boot timeout; the Startup tab was removed from msconfig in Windows 8 and later and relocated to Task Manager."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.3 — Windows Tools",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.3 — Windows Tools",messerSection:"1.3",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-1.3-003",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.3",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, use features and tools of the Microsoft Windows OS.",question:"A technician suspects Windows system files became corrupted after a failed update. Which command scans all protected Windows system files and automatically replaces corrupted files with cached good copies?",options:["chkdsk /f","defrag C: /u","sfc /scannow","DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /ScanHealth"],correct:2,explanation:"'sfc /scannow' (System File Checker) scans all protected Windows system files for integrity violations and repairs corrupted or missing files using cached copies from the component store (%WinDir%\\System32\\dllcache). It must be run from an elevated (Administrator) command prompt. If sfc cannot repair files, run DISM /RestoreHealth first, then re-run sfc.",wrongReasons:["chkdsk /f checks the file system structure and disk surface for errors; it does not repair Windows OS protected system files.","defrag defragments hard drive data for better performance; it has no role in repairing corrupted Windows system file integrity.","null","DISM /ScanHealth checks the component store for corruption without repairing it; DISM /RestoreHealth repairs the store, typically followed by sfc /scannow."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.3 — Windows Tools",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.3 — Windows Command-Line Tools",messerSection:"1.3",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-1.3-004",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.3",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, use features and tools of the Microsoft Windows OS.",question:"A technician needs to investigate application crashes, failed services, and security events on a Windows workstation. Which Windows built-in tool provides searchable, categorized logs of System, Application, and Security events?",options:["Task Manager","Resource Monitor","Event Viewer","Performance Monitor"],correct:2,explanation:"Event Viewer (eventvwr.msc) displays structured event logs organized by category: System (OS and driver events), Application (software events), Security (logon and audit events), and Setup. Each event includes severity level (Information, Warning, Error, Critical), timestamp, source, event ID, and detailed description — essential for troubleshooting and forensic analysis.",wrongReasons:["Task Manager shows running processes, CPU/memory/disk usage, and startup programs; it does not provide historical event logs.","Resource Monitor shows real-time CPU, memory, disk, and network utilization with process-level detail; it does not provide historical event logs.","null","Performance Monitor (perfmon.exe) records and graphs performance counter data over time; it does not show system/application/security event logs."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.3 — Windows Tools",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.3 — Windows Tools",messerSection:"1.3",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-1.4-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.4",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, use the appropriate Windows settings.",question:"A technician installs a new 3 TB hard drive in a UEFI-based Windows 11 system. Which partition style must be used so the full 3 TB capacity is accessible?",options:["MBR (Master Boot Record)","GPT (GUID Partition Table)","FAT32 partition only","Dynamic disk"],correct:1,explanation:"GPT (GUID Partition Table) supports drives larger than 2 TB (MBR's maximum with 512-byte sectors) and up to 128 partitions on Windows. UEFI firmware is required to boot from a GPT system disk. A 3 TB drive initialized as MBR would only expose approximately 2 TB of usable space.",wrongReasons:["MBR is limited to 2 TB maximum addressable space; a 3 TB MBR drive would lose approximately 1 TB of capacity.","null","FAT32 is a file system type, not a partition style; it also has a 32 GB volume limit on Windows (though the specification technically allows larger).","Dynamic disks are a Windows-specific disk management feature for volumes spanning multiple disks; they use either MBR or GPT as their underlying partition style."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.4 — Disk Management",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.4 — Disk Management",messerSection:"1.4",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-1.4-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.4",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, use the appropriate Windows settings.",question:"A company wants to protect sensitive data on employee laptops by encrypting the entire OS drive using the TPM to seal the encryption key. Which Windows Pro/Enterprise feature provides this full-drive encryption?",options:["EFS (Encrypting File System)","BitLocker Drive Encryption","Windows Defender SmartScreen","Storage Spaces"],correct:1,explanation:"BitLocker Drive Encryption encrypts entire drive volumes using AES. When protecting the OS drive, it uses the TPM to seal the encryption key — if the system's boot environment changes (e.g., BIOS tampered with), BitLocker withholds the key. A stolen drive without the recovery key cannot be decrypted.",wrongReasons:["EFS (Encrypting File System) encrypts individual files and folders selected by the user; it does not encrypt the entire drive and does not protect against removing the drive and mounting it elsewhere.","null","Windows Defender SmartScreen filters malicious websites and downloads; it provides no drive encryption.","Storage Spaces pools multiple drives into virtual disks for redundancy or capacity; it does not provide encryption."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.4 — Windows Features",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.4 — Windows Features",messerSection:"1.4",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-1.4-003",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.4",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, use the appropriate Windows settings.",question:"A company's workstations run an OS version Microsoft no longer supports with security patches. What is the PRIMARY security risk of running an End of Life (EOL) operating system?",options:["The OS will begin deleting user files automatically","System performance immediately degrades after EOL date","Discovered security vulnerabilities receive no patches, leaving the system permanently exposed","Microsoft remotely disables EOL systems"],correct:2,explanation:"After EOL, Microsoft stops releasing security updates. Any vulnerabilities discovered after the EOL date remain unpatched forever, giving attackers a permanent advantage. Organizations also face compliance violations in regulated industries (HIPAA, PCI DSS) when running EOL software.",wrongReasons:["Microsoft does not delete user files when an OS reaches EOL; existing data and functionality remain intact.","System performance does not change on the EOL date; existing functionality continues until specific compatibility breaks or hardware fails.","null","Microsoft does not remotely disable EOL systems; they continue to operate without security patches."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.4 — OS Lifecycle",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.4 — Windows Features",messerSection:"1.4",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-1.4-004",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.4",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, use the appropriate Windows settings.",question:"An IT department manages a network where all user and computer authentication is centralized, Group Policy settings are pushed to all machines automatically, and administrators manage resources from a single location. What network model does this describe?",options:["Workgroup","HomeGroup","Domain","Mesh network"],correct:2,explanation:"A domain is a centralized network management model built around a Windows Server Domain Controller. The DC authenticates all users and computers, Group Policy Objects (GPOs) enforce security and configuration settings across all domain members, and administrators manage resources (users, permissions, software) centrally. This contrasts with workgroups where each PC manages its own accounts independently.",wrongReasons:["A workgroup is a peer-to-peer model with no central authentication; each computer maintains its own user account database and manages its own resources independently.","HomeGroup was a simple file sharing feature removed in Windows 10 version 1803; it provided no centralized authentication or Group Policy management.","null","A mesh network is a wireless topology where nodes connect to each other for coverage and redundancy; it is a network architecture concept, not an OS management model."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.4 — Network Configuration",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.4 — Windows Features",messerSection:"1.4",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-1.5-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.5",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, troubleshoot issues with macOS features and tools.",question:"A macOS user wants to encrypt their entire internal drive so all data is protected if the Mac is stolen. Which built-in macOS feature provides full-disk encryption?",options:["Time Machine","Keychain Access","FileVault","Gatekeeper"],correct:2,explanation:"FileVault is macOS's full-disk encryption feature, encrypting the startup disk with XTS-AES-128. On Macs with Apple silicon or T2 chip, storage is hardware-encrypted at rest; FileVault adds user-based key protection requiring authentication to unlock. A stolen encrypted drive cannot be read on another system without the recovery key.",wrongReasons:["Time Machine is macOS's incremental backup system creating snapshots of the system; it does not encrypt the primary startup disk.","Keychain Access manages stored passwords, encryption keys, and certificates; it does not provide full-disk encryption.","null","Gatekeeper verifies that downloaded applications come from identified developers and are free of malware; it provides no disk encryption."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.5 — macOS Features",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.5 — macOS",messerSection:"1.5",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-1.7-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.7",objectiveText:"Identify common features and tools of the Linux OS.",question:"A Linux administrator needs to install a new application package on a Red Hat Enterprise Linux 8 system. Which package management command should they use?",options:["apt install","yum install","dnf install","zypper install"],correct:2,explanation:"DNF (Dandified YUM) replaced yum as the default package manager starting with RHEL 8, CentOS Stream 8, and Fedora. While typing 'yum' on RHEL 8+ redirects to dnf for backward compatibility, dnf is the correct and current tool. For the CompTIA A+ 220-1202 V15 exam objectives, dnf is the expected answer for Red Hat-based distributions.",wrongReasons:["apt is the package manager for Debian-based distributions (Ubuntu, Debian, Linux Mint); it is not used on Red Hat-based systems.","yum was the package manager for RHEL 7 and earlier; it was replaced by dnf in RHEL 8 and is no longer the primary tool for current systems.","null","zypper is the package manager for SUSE Linux Enterprise and openSUSE; it is not used on Red Hat-based distributions."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.7 — Linux OS",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.7 — Linux Features",messerSection:"1.7",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-1.7-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.7",objectiveText:"Identify common features and tools of the Linux OS.",question:"A Linux file system became corrupted after an unexpected power outage. Which command checks and repairs the file system (run on an unmounted partition)?",options:["fdisk -l","mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdb1","fsck /dev/sdb1","mount -a"],correct:2,explanation:"fsck (File System Consistency Check) scans and repairs Linux file systems. It must be run on an unmounted partition — running fsck on a mounted partition risks additional corruption. fsck checks for inode errors, block inconsistencies, bad blocks, and directory structure issues. Some distributions automatically run fsck at boot when a dirty file system is detected.",wrongReasons:["fdisk -l lists partition table information on disks; it does not check or repair file system integrity.","mkfs.ext4 formats a partition with a brand-new ext4 file system, destroying all existing data in the process; it is not a repair tool.","null","mount -a mounts all file systems listed in /etc/fstab; it cannot repair a corrupted file system."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.7 — Linux OS",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.7 — Linux Commands",messerSection:"1.7",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-1.8-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"1.0",domainName:"Operating Systems",objective:"1.8",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, troubleshoot common issues in mobile operating systems.",question:"Apple releases an urgent security fix for iPhones that installs in minutes without a full iOS version update and without requiring a reboot. Which iOS feature delivers this targeted security patch?",options:["TestFlight beta update","Full iOS major version upgrade","Rapid Security Response (RSR)","App Store automatic update"],correct:2,explanation:"Rapid Security Response (RSR), introduced in iOS 16.4.1a and macOS 13.3.1a, delivers critical security patches outside the regular OS update cycle. RSR patches are small files that install quickly, typically require no reboot (or only a brief restart), and can be removed if they cause compatibility problems. They address targeted exploits between regular update cycles.",wrongReasons:["TestFlight is Apple's beta testing platform for developers testing pre-release apps; it is not a mechanism for delivering OS security patches.","A full iOS major version upgrade requires significant download time and a complete device reboot; RSR patches are specifically designed to be fast and minimally disruptive.","null","App Store automatic updates keep installed third-party apps current; they do not deliver OS-level security patches."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 1.8 — Mobile OS",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 1.8 — Mobile Operating Systems",messerSection:"1.8",difficulty:"medium"},
// Domain 2.0 Security
{id:"c2-2.1-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.1",objectiveText:"Summarize various security measures and their purposes.",question:"A security team implements a policy where no user or device is automatically trusted — even inside the corporate network. Every access request requires continuous verification regardless of origin. Which security model does this describe?",options:["Defense in depth","Zero Trust","DMZ architecture","VPN-only access"],correct:1,explanation:"Zero Trust is built on 'never trust, always verify.' Every user, device, and request — inside or outside the network perimeter — must be authenticated, authorized, and continuously validated. Zero Trust uses identity verification, device health checks, least-privilege access, and micro-segmentation. It assumes breach and limits lateral movement.",wrongReasons:["Defense in depth uses multiple overlapping security layers (firewall, IDS, encryption); it still may grant implicit trust to internal network resources, unlike Zero Trust.","null","A DMZ places public-facing servers in a segment between two firewalls; it is a network architecture element, not an overarching trust model for all access decisions.","VPN-only access requires remote users to connect via VPN; once connected, users typically receive elevated internal trust — the opposite of Zero Trust principles."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.1 — Security Concepts",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.1 — Security Fundamentals",messerSection:"2.1",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-2.1-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.1",objectiveText:"Summarize various security measures and their purposes.",question:"A company requires employees to enter both their password AND a 6-digit code from an authenticator app before accessing corporate email. Which security mechanism does this implement?",options:["Single sign-on (SSO)","Multi-factor authentication (MFA)","RADIUS network authentication","Kerberos ticket-granting"],correct:1,explanation:"MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication) requires two or more verification factors from different categories: something you know (password), something you have (authenticator app / hardware token), and something you are (biometric). Combining a password with an authenticator app code satisfies the 'know + have' requirement.",wrongReasons:["SSO allows one authentication event to access multiple applications; it describes an access pattern, not the requirement for multiple factor types.","null","RADIUS is a network authentication protocol used for centralized access control (VPN, Wi-Fi); it is the infrastructure, not the specific multi-factor combination being described.","Kerberos is a network authentication protocol using encrypted tickets; it describes the mechanism, not the factor-combination requirement."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.1 — Security Concepts",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.1 — Security Fundamentals",messerSection:"2.1",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-2.1-003",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.1",objectiveText:"Summarize various security measures and their purposes.",question:"A user's authenticator app displays a 6-digit code that changes every 30 seconds, calculated from a shared secret and the current time. They enter this code along with their password to log in. Which MFA technology generates this code?",options:["SMS OTP sent by the server","TOTP (Time-Based One-Time Password)","FIDO2 passkey","Push notification approval"],correct:1,explanation:"TOTP (Time-Based One-Time Password), defined in RFC 6238, generates a code by combining the current time with a shared secret key stored on the device. Both the authenticator app and the server independently calculate the same code at the same moment. Codes expire every 30 seconds. Google Authenticator, Authy, and Microsoft Authenticator all implement TOTP.",wrongReasons:["SMS OTP transmits a server-generated code over the cellular network to the user's phone; the code is not calculated on the device from a shared secret, making it less secure than TOTP.","null","FIDO2 passkeys use public-key cryptography and local device authentication (biometric or PIN); they do not generate numeric time-based codes.","Push notification approval (like Duo Push) sends an approve/deny prompt to the user's device; it does not generate a numeric code that the user types."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.1 — Security Concepts",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.1 — Security Fundamentals",messerSection:"2.1",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-2.1-004",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.1",objectiveText:"Summarize various security measures and their purposes.",question:"A Windows 11 laptop allows users to sign in using their fingerprint or face scan, storing biometric data securely in the TPM and never transmitting it over the network. Which Microsoft authentication technology enables this?",options:["Smart card authentication","LDAP directory authentication","Windows Hello","BitLocker pre-boot PIN"],correct:2,explanation:"Windows Hello is Microsoft's biometric authentication framework allowing sign-in via face recognition (IR camera), fingerprint scan, or PIN. Biometric data is processed and stored locally in the TPM — it never leaves the device. Windows Hello meets FIDO2 standards for passwordless and phishing-resistant authentication.",wrongReasons:["Smart card authentication requires a physical card with an embedded certificate chip and a card reader; it does not use biometrics.","LDAP is a directory access protocol for authenticating against directory servers; it is not a biometric login framework.","null","BitLocker pre-boot PIN unlocks the encrypted drive before Windows loads; it uses a numeric PIN, not biometrics, and is a disk protection mechanism rather than a user login method."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.1 — Security Concepts",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.1 — Security Fundamentals",messerSection:"2.1",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-2.2-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.2",objectiveText:"Compare and contrast wireless security protocols and authentication methods.",question:"A network administrator upgrades wireless security to a protocol that uses SAE (Simultaneous Authentication of Equals) instead of a shared pre-shared key handshake, providing perfect forward secrecy and resistance to offline dictionary attacks. Which protocol is this?",options:["WEP","WPA","WPA2","WPA3"],correct:3,explanation:"WPA3 replaces WPA2's PSK handshake with SAE (also called the Dragonfly handshake). SAE provides perfect forward secrecy — each session derives unique keys — and prevents offline dictionary attacks because an attacker cannot capture a handshake and test passwords offline. WPA3 is defined in IEEE 802.11ax and is mandatory for Wi-Fi 6 certification.",wrongReasons:["WEP (Wired Equivalent Privacy) is completely broken due to its static key and RC4 implementation flaws; it can be cracked in minutes.","WPA used TKIP as a temporary fix for WEP; TKIP is also deprecated and considered insecure.","WPA2 uses the 4-way handshake with PBKDF2 which is vulnerable to offline dictionary attacks against captured handshakes; WPA3 fixes this with SAE.",null],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.2 — Wireless Security",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.2 — Wireless Security",messerSection:"2.2",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-2.2-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.2",objectiveText:"Compare and contrast wireless security protocols and authentication methods.",question:"A privacy-conscious user configures their browser to send DNS queries encrypted over HTTPS to a trusted resolver on TCP port 443, preventing their ISP from seeing which domains they visit. Which protocol does this describe?",options:["DNSSEC","DoT (DNS over TLS)","DoH (DNS over HTTPS)","Split-tunnel VPN"],correct:2,explanation:"DNS over HTTPS (DoH) encapsulates DNS queries inside HTTPS traffic on TCP port 443. This encrypts DNS lookups so that ISPs, network administrators, and eavesdroppers cannot see which domains are being resolved. Both Firefox and Chrome support DoH natively. Using port 443 makes DoH traffic indistinguishable from regular HTTPS.",wrongReasons:["DNSSEC adds cryptographic signatures to DNS records to verify their authenticity against tampering; it does not encrypt the DNS queries themselves — they remain visible in plaintext.","DoT (DNS over TLS) also encrypts DNS queries but uses TCP port 853, which is easily identified and filtered; DoH uses port 443 making it harder to block.","null","Split-tunnel VPN routes some traffic through the VPN and some directly; it is a VPN configuration, not a DNS encryption protocol."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.2 — Security Protocols",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.2 — Security Protocols",messerSection:"2.2",difficulty:"hard"},
{id:"c2-2.3-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.3",objectiveText:"Explain common physical security measures.",question:"A data center entry requires employees to badge into a small enclosed room with two interlocked doors — the outer door must fully close and lock before the inner door can open. This prevents unauthorized individuals from slipping in with authorized users. What is this physical security control called?",options:["Biometric lock","Badge reader","Access control vestibule","Security camera system"],correct:2,explanation:"An access control vestibule (formerly called a mantrap in older documentation, but the current CompTIA 220-1202 objectives use 'access control vestibule') uses two interlocked doors where only one can be open at a time. After the outer door closes, identity is verified before the inner door unlocks. This physically prevents tailgating/piggybacking into secured areas.",wrongReasons:["A biometric lock is an authentication technology (fingerprint or face scan) that may be installed on a vestibule door; it is one component, not the two-door physical structure itself.","A badge reader authenticates using RFID or smart cards; it is a component that can be used as part of a vestibule, not the two-door physical enclosure.","null","Security cameras record and monitor activity; they do not physically prevent tailgating as the vestibule does — cameras are detective controls, not preventive ones."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.3 — Physical Security",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.3 — Physical Security",messerSection:"2.3",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-2.3-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.3",objectiveText:"Explain common physical security measures.",question:"An unauthorized person waits near a secured door and slips inside immediately behind an authorized employee who badges in, without independently authenticating. What is this physical security threat called?",options:["Shoulder surfing","Tailgating (piggybacking)","Dumpster diving","Vishing"],correct:1,explanation:"Tailgating (also called piggybacking) occurs when an unauthorized person follows closely behind an authorized user through a secured door without independently authenticating. It exploits social norms where people hold doors open. Countermeasures include access control vestibules, security awareness training, and turnstiles that only allow one person per authentication.",wrongReasons:["Shoulder surfing involves observing someone's screen, keyboard, or PIN pad to steal information; it does not involve bypassing physical access controls through doors.","null","Dumpster diving involves searching through discarded materials (waste, recycling) for sensitive information such as documents or hardware; it is not a physical access bypass technique.","Vishing uses phone calls to manipulate victims into revealing sensitive information; it has no physical access component."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.3 — Physical Security",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.3 — Physical Security",messerSection:"2.3",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-2.4-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.4",objectiveText:"Explain common threats, vulnerabilities, and attack types.",question:"A security team deploys a solution that continuously monitors all endpoints for suspicious behavior, automatically isolates compromised devices, and provides forensic investigation tools. Which security technology does this describe?",options:["Signature-based antivirus","Stateful firewall with IPS","EDR (Endpoint Detection and Response)","Password manager with breach monitoring"],correct:2,explanation:"EDR (Endpoint Detection and Response) monitors endpoint activity continuously using behavioral analysis to detect threats that signature-based tools miss (including zero-days and fileless malware). EDR can automatically isolate compromised endpoints, provides forensic investigation capabilities, and generates detailed telemetry. Extended Detection and Response (XDR) expands EDR across email, cloud, and network layers.",wrongReasons:["Signature-based antivirus detects known malware by matching file hashes; it cannot detect behavioral threats, zero-days, or fileless malware with no known signature.","A stateful firewall with IPS monitors and filters network traffic; it lacks deep endpoint visibility into process behavior, memory access, and file system activity.","null","A password manager with breach monitoring protects credentials; it has no role in endpoint threat detection or incident response."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.4 — Security Tools",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.4 — Security Threats",messerSection:"2.4",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-2.4-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.4",objectiveText:"Explain common threats, vulnerabilities, and attack types.",question:"A compromised Windows system never had any malicious files written to disk. The attack executed entirely in RAM by injecting code into legitimate processes like explorer.exe and lsass.exe, leaving no file artifacts. Which type of malware is this?",options:["Rootkit","Ransomware","Fileless malware","Spyware"],correct:2,explanation:"Fileless malware executes entirely in memory (RAM) without writing malicious files to disk. It abuses legitimate system tools — PowerShell, WMI, the Windows registry, or legitimate process injection — to run malicious code. Because no malicious files exist on disk, traditional file-scanning antivirus cannot detect it. EDR tools with memory analysis are required.",wrongReasons:["A rootkit hides itself and other malware at the OS kernel or bootloader level; it typically modifies files on disk or hooks into kernel structures.","Ransomware encrypts the victim's files and writes ransom notes to disk; it necessarily creates file artifacts.","null","Spyware monitors user activity and exfiltrates data; while it can be stealthy, it typically writes persistent files to disk to survive reboots."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.4 — Malware Types",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.4 — Malware",messerSection:"2.4",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-2.4-003",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.4",objectiveText:"Explain common threats, vulnerabilities, and attack types.",question:"A victim's files — documents, photos, and databases — have been encrypted and made inaccessible. A message demands cryptocurrency payment in exchange for the decryption key. Which malware type is responsible?",options:["Adware","Spyware","Ransomware","Rootkit"],correct:2,explanation:"Ransomware encrypts victim files using asymmetric encryption — the attacker holds the private decryption key and demands payment (typically cryptocurrency) to provide it. Even if the malware is removed, encrypted files remain inaccessible without the key. Offline backups following the 3-2-1 rule are the most effective defense against ransomware.",wrongReasons:["Adware displays unwanted advertisements to generate revenue; it does not encrypt files or demand payment.","Spyware monitors user activity and steals data (keystrokes, credentials, browsing history); it does not encrypt files or demand ransoms.","null","A rootkit hides malware components from security tools by operating at kernel level; while it may deliver other malware, rootkits do not specifically encrypt user files and demand ransom."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.4 — Malware Types",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.4 — Malware",messerSection:"2.4",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-2.4-004",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.4",objectiveText:"Explain common threats, vulnerabilities, and attack types.",question:"Software was covertly installed on a person's smartphone by someone with physical access to the device. It secretly tracks their GPS location, records calls, and reads messages — designed to surveil the victim without their knowledge. Which type of malicious software is this?",options:["Adware","Stalkerware","Trojan horse","Ransomware"],correct:1,explanation:"Stalkerware (also called spouseware or creepware) is covertly installed software designed to surveil a victim's location, communications, calls, messages, and photos — typically by an intimate partner or abuser. Unlike generic spyware, stalkerware is specifically designed for covert personal surveillance of an individual and often hides its icon to avoid detection.",wrongReasons:["Adware generates revenue by displaying unwanted advertisements; it does not covertly surveil location, calls, or personal communications.","null","A Trojan horse disguises itself as legitimate software to gain access; while it can deliver stalkerware, the term for the surveillance functionality itself is stalkerware.","Ransomware encrypts files and demands payment; it does not monitor or track an individual's personal communications and location."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.4 — Malware Types",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.4 — Malware",messerSection:"2.4",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-2.5-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.5",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, detect, remove, and prevent malware using appropriate tools and methods.",question:"An attacker sends a meticulously crafted email to a company's CEO impersonating their law firm and requesting an urgent wire transfer, using details from public filings. This targeted attack against a senior executive is called what?",options:["Generic phishing","Vishing","Whaling","Smishing"],correct:2,explanation:"Whaling is a highly targeted form of phishing that specifically attacks senior executives (C-suite: CEO, CFO, CTO, COO). Attackers research the target and company extensively, using personalized details and high-stakes scenarios (legal matters, wire transfers, board actions) that exploit the executive's authority and sense of urgency.",wrongReasons:["Generic phishing targets a broad audience with bulk messages using generic lures; it is not personalized and does not specifically target senior executives.","Vishing uses voice telephone calls to deceive victims; it is not an email-based attack targeting executives.","null","Smishing uses SMS text messages as the attack vector; it is not targeted at senior executives via email."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.5 — Social Engineering",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.5 — Social Engineering",messerSection:"2.5",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-2.5-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.5",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, detect, remove, and prevent malware using appropriate tools and methods.",question:"An attacker sets up a rogue wireless access point with the same SSID as a coffee shop's legitimate network. Customers unknowingly connect to the attacker's AP, which intercepts all their traffic. What is this attack called?",options:["Deauthentication flood","Replay attack","Evil twin attack","Wardriving"],correct:2,explanation:"An evil twin attack creates a fraudulent wireless access point that mimics a legitimate network's SSID (and optionally its BSSID/MAC address), often with a stronger signal. Victims connect to the attacker's AP enabling man-in-the-middle attacks, credential theft, and traffic interception. Using a VPN on public Wi-Fi mitigates this risk.",wrongReasons:["A deauthentication flood sends forged 802.11 deauthentication frames to disconnect clients from a legitimate AP; while it can force clients onto an evil twin, it is a separate attack technique.","A replay attack captures legitimate authentication packets and re-sends them to impersonate a user; it operates at the protocol level, not by creating fake APs.","null","Wardriving involves physically moving through an area scanning for wireless networks; it is a reconnaissance activity, not the act of creating a fraudulent AP to intercept traffic."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.5 — Social Engineering",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.5 — Wireless Attacks",messerSection:"2.5",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-2.5-003",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.5",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, detect, remove, and prevent malware using appropriate tools and methods.",question:"A finance department receives email from what appears to be their CEO's real email address requesting an urgent $50,000 wire transfer. Investigation reveals the CEO's account was not compromised — attackers spoofed the domain to make it appear legitimate. What type of attack is this?",options:["Ransomware delivery","Spear phishing for credentials","BEC (Business Email Compromise)","Credential stuffing"],correct:2,explanation:"BEC (Business Email Compromise) is a fraud attack where attackers impersonate executives or trusted partners via email — either by compromising their actual account or spoofing their domain — to trick employees into authorizing fraudulent financial transactions or sensitive data transfers. BEC attacks are primarily financially motivated and cause billions in annual losses.",wrongReasons:["Ransomware encrypts data for extortion; BEC is a financial fraud targeting wire transfers and does not involve data encryption.","Spear phishing targets individuals to steal credentials or install malware; BEC specifically impersonates trusted authority figures to authorize fraudulent transactions, making BEC the most accurate classification.","null","Credential stuffing uses leaked username/password lists to attempt logins; this attack does not involve account compromise — it uses domain spoofing for financial fraud."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.5 — Social Engineering",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.5 — Social Engineering",messerSection:"2.5",difficulty:"hard"},
{id:"c2-2.5-004",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.5",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, detect, remove, and prevent malware using appropriate tools and methods.",question:"A user scans a QR code from a poster at a transit station and is redirected to a fake login page that steals their credentials. The URL embedded in the QR code was not visible before scanning. What type of attack is this?",options:["Vishing","Smishing","Quishing","Spear phishing"],correct:2,explanation:"Quishing (QR code phishing) embeds malicious URLs inside QR codes rather than text hyperlinks. Because the URL is hidden within the image, standard email security tools and users cannot inspect the link before scanning. Victims are directed to phishing sites that steal credentials or deliver malware. Quishing campaigns have targeted parking meters, restaurant menus, and public signage.",wrongReasons:["Vishing uses telephone calls or voice messages as the attack vector; QR codes are not involved.","Smishing uses SMS text messages as the phishing vector; while a text could contain a QR code link, the term 'quishing' specifically describes phishing via QR codes.","null","Spear phishing is a targeted attack using personalized information against specific individuals; publicly posted QR codes targeting random victims is a broadcast attack, not spear phishing."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.5 — Social Engineering",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.5 — Social Engineering",messerSection:"2.5",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-2.5-005",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.5",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, detect, remove, and prevent malware using appropriate tools and methods.",question:"Security researchers discover that a widely used software library contained malicious code inserted during the build process by an attacker who compromised a developer's account. Any application using this library unknowingly distributed the malware. Which attack type is this?",options:["Zero-day exploit","Man-in-the-middle attack","Supply chain attack","Watering hole attack"],correct:2,explanation:"A supply chain attack compromises targets indirectly by inserting malicious code into a trusted component — a software library, update server, build system, or hardware supply chain — that the target organization uses. The victim trusts and installs the compromised component. The SolarWinds Orion and XZ Utils (CVE-2024-3094) incidents are real-world examples.",wrongReasons:["A zero-day exploit leverages a previously unknown software vulnerability; it does not involve tampering with the development or distribution pipeline of a dependency.","A man-in-the-middle attack intercepts communication between parties in real-time; it does not involve inserting malicious code into a development pipeline.","null","A watering hole attack compromises websites frequented by target groups; it does not involve tampering with software libraries or build systems."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.5 — Attack Types",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.5 — Attack Types",messerSection:"2.5",difficulty:"hard"},
{id:"c2-2.6-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"2.0",domainName:"Security",objective:"2.6",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, detect, remove, and prevent malware using appropriate tools and methods.",question:"A technician follows the CompTIA A+ 220-1202 malware removal process. After identifying malware symptoms and researching the malware type, what is the NEXT step before beginning removal?",options:["Update antivirus definitions","Run a full system scan immediately","Quarantine the infected system from the network","Restore the system from backup"],correct:2,explanation:"The CompTIA A+ 220-1202 malware removal process (10 steps): 1) Identify and research symptoms; 2) Quarantine the infected system — disconnect from the network and disable Wi-Fi/Bluetooth to prevent spread and stop command-and-control communication; 3) Disable System Restore; 4) Remediate; 5) Update security software; 6) Run scans; 7) Schedule scans; 8) Enable System Restore and create restore point; 9) Educate end user; 10) Document findings. Quarantine comes before scanning.",wrongReasons:["Updating antivirus definitions is step 5 (after quarantine and disabling System Restore); updating definitions on a connected infected system risks further spread.","Running a full scan is step 6; the system must be quarantined first to isolate it and prevent C2 communication or network propagation.","null","Restoring from backup is a recovery option when removal fails; it is not the next step after identifying symptoms."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 2.6 — Malware Removal",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 2.6 — Malware Removal",messerSection:"2.6",difficulty:"medium"},
// Domain 3.0 Software Troubleshooting
{id:"c2-3.1-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"3.0",domainName:"Software Troubleshooting",objective:"3.1",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, troubleshoot common Windows OS problems.",question:"A Windows 10 PC displays 'BOOTMGR is missing — Press Ctrl+Alt+Del to restart' on every boot. The hard drive tests as healthy. What is the MOST appropriate first recovery step?",options:["Replace the hard drive","Reinstall Windows from scratch","Boot from Windows installation media and run Startup Repair","Run chkdsk from within the running Windows OS"],correct:2,explanation:"'BOOTMGR is missing' means the Windows Boot Manager files on the system partition are corrupted or missing. Booting from Windows installation or recovery media and selecting Startup Repair (Automatic Repair) can rebuild the BCD (Boot Configuration Data) store and restore BOOTMGR without destroying user data. This is the correct first step before considering reinstallation.",wrongReasons:["The hard drive is confirmed healthy; replacing it would not restore the missing boot manager files.","A fresh reinstall destroys user data; Startup Repair should be attempted first since it can resolve boot manager issues without data loss.","null","chkdsk requires a functioning Windows environment or must be run from recovery media; if BOOTMGR is missing, Windows cannot boot to run chkdsk normally."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 3.1 — Windows Troubleshooting",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 3.1 — Windows Troubleshooting",messerSection:"3.1",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-3.1-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"3.0",domainName:"Software Troubleshooting",objective:"3.1",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, troubleshoot common Windows OS problems.",question:"A Windows PC runs extremely slowly. Task Manager shows Disk at 100% consistently, but CPU and RAM usage are low. The HDD SMART status is healthy. What is the MOST likely software cause?",options:["Failing RAM causing excessive paging","Too many browser tabs open","Windows Search indexing or SysMain (Superfetch) service causing heavy disk I/O","Overheating CPU throttling to minimum speed"],correct:2,explanation:"Constant 100% disk usage on a healthy HDD is frequently caused by Windows background services — particularly Windows Search (content indexing) or SysMain/Superfetch (preloading applications into RAM). On HDDs (especially with limited RAM), these services generate sustained heavy disk I/O. Disabling SysMain via Services.msc or adjusting Windows Search indexing scope typically resolves this.",wrongReasons:["Failing RAM would cause BSODs, application crashes, and visible memory pressure in Task Manager; it would not cause isolated 100% disk usage while RAM and CPU usage remain low.","Browser tabs consume RAM and CPU but do not cause system-wide 100% disk utilization across all processes.","null","CPU throttling from overheating would show reduced CPU frequencies and high temperatures; disk usage would not be 100% while CPU usage is normal."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 3.1 — Windows Troubleshooting",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 3.1 — OS Troubleshooting",messerSection:"3.1",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-3.1-003",exam:"core2",domainNum:"3.0",domainName:"Software Troubleshooting",objective:"3.1",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, troubleshoot common Windows OS problems.",question:"A computer BSODs immediately after a Windows Update is applied and cannot boot to Windows. What is the BEST approach to recover user data and fix the boot problem?",options:["Format the drive and reinstall Windows","Boot into Windows Recovery Environment and use Uninstall Updates","Delete the Windows folder manually","Run sfc /scannow from the login screen"],correct:1,explanation:"When a Windows Update causes a boot-preventing BSOD, boot into Windows Recovery Environment (WinRE) via F8/Shift+Restart or installation media, then navigate to Troubleshoot > Advanced Options > Uninstall Updates. This removes the most recent quality or feature update while preserving user files and applications.",wrongReasons:["Formatting and reinstalling destroys all user data; this is a last resort, not the first response to an update-caused boot failure.","null","Deleting the Windows folder makes the system completely unbootable and destroys system files; it is not a recovery technique.","sfc /scannow requires an elevated command prompt within a running Windows session; it cannot be run from the login screen when the system cannot boot past the BSOD."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 3.1 — Windows Troubleshooting",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 3.1 — Windows Troubleshooting",messerSection:"3.1",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-3.1-004",exam:"core2",domainNum:"3.0",domainName:"Software Troubleshooting",objective:"3.1",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, troubleshoot common Windows OS problems.",question:"A Windows computer displays an 'INACCESSIBLE_BOOT_DEVICE' BSOD. The HDD SMART status is healthy. What is the MOST likely cause?",options:["Corrupted user profile","Missing application DLL","Corrupted or incompatible storage controller driver (e.g., NVMe/AHCI mode changed)","Outdated video card driver"],correct:2,explanation:"INACCESSIBLE_BOOT_DEVICE indicates Windows cannot access the boot volume at startup. Common causes include changing the storage controller mode in BIOS/UEFI (e.g., switching from AHCI to IDE/RAID), a corrupted or missing NVMe driver after an update, or a corrupted BCD. Recovery options include Startup Repair from WinRE or rolling back storage drivers.",wrongReasons:["A corrupted user profile causes errors after successful Windows login (default profile loads instead of the user's); it does not prevent the OS from accessing the boot volume.","A missing DLL causes application-specific errors or post-boot instability; it does not prevent Windows from accessing the boot device at a pre-login stage.","null","An outdated video driver causes display artifacts, black screens, or VIDEO_TDR_FAILURE BSODs; it does not cause INACCESSIBLE_BOOT_DEVICE."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 3.1 — BSOD Troubleshooting",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 3.1 — Windows BSOD",messerSection:"3.1",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-3.2-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"3.0",domainName:"Software Troubleshooting",objective:"3.2",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, troubleshoot common personal computer security issues.",question:"A user's browser homepage changed to an unknown search engine without their consent, new toolbar extensions appeared, and search results redirect to unexpected sites. Which type of malware is responsible?",options:["Ransomware","Rootkit","Browser hijacker","Keylogger"],correct:2,explanation:"A browser hijacker modifies browser settings without consent — changing the homepage, default search engine, and installing unwanted extensions. It redirects searches to generate advertising revenue or collect search data. Browser hijackers are often bundled with free software. Removal requires anti-malware scanning and manually resetting browser settings to defaults.",wrongReasons:["Ransomware encrypts user files and demands payment; it does not change browser settings or install toolbar extensions.","A rootkit hides malware from the OS and security tools at the kernel level; while it may enable other malware, it does not specifically cause browser homepage changes and toolbar installations.","null","A keylogger records keystrokes to steal credentials; it operates silently without changing browser settings or installing visible extensions."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 3.2 — Security Troubleshooting",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 3.2 — Security Troubleshooting",messerSection:"3.2",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-3.2-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"3.0",domainName:"Software Troubleshooting",objective:"3.2",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, troubleshoot common personal computer security issues.",question:"A technician tries to install antivirus software on a malware-infected PC but the installer immediately fails. When they launch an existing security tool, its window disappears within seconds. What tactic is the malware using?",options:["Ransomware encryption preventing file writes","The technician downloaded a corrupt installer","Malware actively terminating security software processes to prevent detection","The antivirus license has expired"],correct:2,explanation:"Some advanced malware actively monitors for security software processes and terminates them — a self-defense technique used by sophisticated trojans and rootkits to prevent removal. To bypass this, boot from external rescue media (a bootable antivirus USB drive) or use an offline scanning environment where the malware is not active and cannot interfere.",wrongReasons:["Ransomware encrypts data files; it does not specifically prevent antivirus installation or kill running security tool processes.","A corrupt installer produces installer-specific error messages; a corrupt file would not cause a separately installed tool's window to disappear after launch.","null","An expired license causes license warnings or limited-feature mode; it would not cause a different antivirus product's installer to fail or cause the existing tool's interface to close."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 3.2 — Security Troubleshooting",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 3.2 — Malware Removal",messerSection:"3.2",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-3.3-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"3.0",domainName:"Software Troubleshooting",objective:"3.3",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, troubleshoot common mobile OS and application issues.",question:"An Android phone's display does not rotate when the device is turned sideways — it stays locked in portrait mode. The user has not reported any drops or physical damage. What is the MOST likely cause?",options:["A cracked digitizer","The accelerometer has failed","Rotation lock (auto-rotate) is enabled in settings","The display needs hardware recalibration"],correct:2,explanation:"Rotation lock (Auto-rotate / Screen rotation) is a software setting that prevents the display from changing orientation. When enabled, the screen stays in one orientation regardless of how the device is held. It can be toggled in the Quick Settings pull-down panel or in Display settings. This is the most common cause and should be checked before suspecting hardware failure.",wrongReasons:["A cracked digitizer affects touch input and screen display; rotation control uses the accelerometer/gyroscope sensors, not the digitizer.","A failed accelerometer would also prevent rotation, but rotation lock is far more common and should always be verified first before diagnosing hardware sensor failure.","null","Display recalibration is not a standard Android procedure and is not related to the rotation function, which is sensor-driven."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 3.3 — Mobile Troubleshooting",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 3.3 — Mobile OS Issues",messerSection:"3.3",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-3.3-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"3.0",domainName:"Software Troubleshooting",objective:"3.3",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, troubleshoot common mobile OS and application issues.",question:"A user installed a flashlight app from a third-party website on their Android phone. Shortly afterward, unauthorized calls are made and contacts are exfiltrated to an unknown server. What MOST likely caused this?",options:["The official Google Play version has the same code","An outdated Android OS created the vulnerability","A malicious app installed via sideloading bypassed app store security vetting","The carrier pushed the malicious app automatically"],correct:2,explanation:"Sideloading installs apps from outside the official app store, bypassing Google Play's security review (Protect scanning, policy checks). Malicious apps distributed this way may request excessive permissions and perform harmful actions — call interception, data exfiltration, premium SMS fraud. Android requires enabling 'Install unknown apps' to sideload, which increases risk significantly.",wrongReasons:["Official app store versions go through security review; the issue directly resulted from installing an unvetted app from outside the official store.","An outdated OS may have exploitable vulnerabilities, but the immediate cause is the installation of a malicious app that bypassed the app store vetting process.","null","Carriers do not silently install third-party applications; the behavior described is consistent with a malicious sideloaded application with excessive permissions."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 3.3 — Mobile Troubleshooting",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 3.3 — Mobile Security",messerSection:"3.3",difficulty:"medium"},
// Domain 4.0 Operational Procedures
{id:"c2-4.1-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"4.0",domainName:"Operational Procedures",objective:"4.1",objectiveText:"Implement best practices associated with documentation and support system tools.",question:"A help desk technician resolves a complex network connectivity issue. To preserve institutional knowledge and enable faster resolution if the issue recurs, what should they create?",options:["A user training video explaining networking basics","Thorough ticket documentation: symptoms, diagnosis steps, root cause, and resolution","A new Group Policy Object to prevent recurrence","A backup of the user's desktop"],correct:1,explanation:"Proper ticket documentation captures reported symptoms, diagnostic steps taken (including what was ruled out), root cause identified, resolution applied, and follow-up actions. This enables other technicians to resolve the same issue more efficiently, supports trend analysis to identify recurring problems, and provides an audit trail.",wrongReasons:["A user training video may help with user education; it does not document the technical troubleshooting steps and resolution for other IT staff.","null","Creating a GPO is a configuration action to prevent certain issues; it does not document the troubleshooting knowledge and resolution details for the specific incident.","A desktop backup protects user data; it does not document the technical issue, its cause, or the resolution steps."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 4.1 — Documentation",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 4.1 — Operational Procedures",messerSection:"4.1",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-4.2-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"4.0",domainName:"Operational Procedures",objective:"4.2",objectiveText:"Explain basic change management best practices.",question:"A critical security vulnerability is being actively exploited in production. The standard change management approval takes 2 weeks. Which change type allows the organization to bypass the standard timeline for urgent situations?",options:["Standard change","Normal change","Emergency change","Routine maintenance window"],correct:2,explanation:"An emergency change provides an expedited approval path for changes that must be implemented immediately due to active security exploits, system outages, or other critical business situations. It still requires documentation and post-implementation review but bypasses the standard scheduling queue. Emergency approval authority may be delegated to on-call management or convene an emergency CAB meeting.",wrongReasons:["A standard change is a pre-approved, low-risk, repeatable change that follows normal procedures; it does not accelerate approval timelines.","A normal change follows the full change management process with defined lead times and CAB approval; it is not designed for urgent situations.","null","A routine maintenance window is a scheduled time for planned work; it does not provide an expedited approval mechanism for emergency security patches."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 4.2 — Change Management",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 4.2 — Change Management",messerSection:"4.2",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-4.3-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"4.0",domainName:"Operational Procedures",objective:"4.3",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, implement workstation backup and recovery methods.",question:"A backup system performs a full backup on Sunday, then incremental backups each weekday. The server fails on Friday afternoon. How many backup sets are required to fully restore to Thursday's state?",options:["Only Thursday's incremental","Only Sunday's full backup","The Sunday full backup plus Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday incrementals","Only the most recent two backups"],correct:2,explanation:"Incremental backups only capture data changed since the LAST backup (whether full or incremental). To restore Thursday: apply Sunday's full backup (baseline), then apply Monday's incremental (adds Monday's changes only), then Tuesday's, then Wednesday's, then Thursday's. Every incremental in the chain is required because each contains only that single day's changes.",wrongReasons:["Thursday's incremental only contains changes since Wednesday; restoring only it would give an incomplete restore missing all earlier changes.","Sunday's full backup restores the system to Sunday's state; all changes from Monday through Thursday would be permanently lost.","null","Each incremental backup only contains changes since the previous backup — not since Sunday's full. Multiple backup sets are always required for incremental restoration."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 4.3 — Backup and Recovery",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 4.3 — Backup Methods",messerSection:"4.3",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-4.3-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"4.0",domainName:"Operational Procedures",objective:"4.3",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, implement workstation backup and recovery methods.",question:"A backup policy requires 3 total copies of data, stored on 2 different media types, with 1 copy kept off-site. Which industry backup strategy is this?",options:["GFS (Grandfather-Father-Son) rotation","Full plus differential backup","3-2-1 backup rule","RAID 5 redundancy"],correct:2,explanation:"The 3-2-1 backup rule is an industry best practice: 3 copies of data (1 primary + 2 backups), on 2 different storage media types (e.g., internal drive + external tape or cloud), with 1 copy stored off-site. The off-site copy ensures survival of local disasters — fire, flood, theft, or ransomware encrypting all local systems simultaneously.",wrongReasons:["GFS (Grandfather-Father-Son) is a backup rotation schedule defining retention periods for daily, weekly, and monthly backups; it does not describe the media distribution and copy-count strategy.","Full plus differential describes backup types (full backup followed by backups of all changes since the last full); it is not a media distribution strategy.","null","RAID 5 provides storage redundancy against drive failure on a single system; it is not a backup strategy and does not protect against accidental deletion, ransomware, or off-site disasters."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 4.3 — Backup Strategies",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 4.3 — Backup Strategies",messerSection:"4.3",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-4.4-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"4.0",domainName:"Operational Procedures",objective:"4.4",objectiveText:"Explain common safety procedures.",question:"Before handling RAM modules, PCIe cards, or a CPU, what should a technician do to prevent electrostatic discharge (ESD) damage to components?",options:["Work on a metal workbench","Wear rubber insulating gloves","Put on an ESD wrist strap connected to a grounded surface","Blow compressed air to discharge static"],correct:2,explanation:"An ESD wrist strap connects the technician's wrist (skin contact) to a grounded reference (chassis or grounding mat) through a 1-megaohm resistor. This continuously equalizes the electrical potential between the technician and sensitive components, preventing the static charge buildup that can permanently damage semiconductor devices such as RAM, CPUs, and PCIe cards.",wrongReasons:["A metal workbench alone is not sufficient — it may actually create a direct discharge path if not bonded to a grounding mat; by itself it does not equalize the technician's potential.","Rubber gloves are insulators that trap static electricity; they can actually make ESD damage more likely by preventing charge dissipation.","null","Compressed air removes dust; it has no effect on the technician's electrostatic charge and does not prevent ESD damage."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 4.4 — Safety Procedures",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 4.4 — Safety Procedures",messerSection:"4.4",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-4.5-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"4.0",domainName:"Operational Procedures",objective:"4.5",objectiveText:"Explain environmental impacts and appropriate controls.",question:"A server room loses utility power. A device connected between the power outlet and the servers immediately provides battery power, allowing systems to continue running and giving time for graceful shutdown or generator start. What is this device?",options:["Surge protector","PDU (Power Distribution Unit)","UPS (Uninterruptible Power Supply)","KVM switch"],correct:2,explanation:"A UPS (Uninterruptible Power Supply) contains batteries that supply power instantaneously when utility power fails — there is no switching delay. This prevents data corruption and hardware damage from sudden power loss. UPS units also condition power (filtering surges, sags, and line noise) and provide runtime to allow safe system shutdown or generator transfer.",wrongReasons:["A surge protector absorbs voltage spikes from the power line but provides zero battery backup; when power fails completely, a surge protector provides no power at all.","A PDU (Power Distribution Unit) distributes power from a single source to multiple receptacles and may include monitoring; it has no battery backup capability.","null","A KVM switch allows one keyboard, monitor, and mouse to control multiple servers; it has no power protection, battery, or UPS functionality."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 4.5 — Environmental Controls",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 4.5 — Environmental Controls",messerSection:"4.5",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-4.6-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"4.0",domainName:"Operational Procedures",objective:"4.6",objectiveText:"Explain the processes for incident response.",question:"A forensic analyst is collecting evidence from a running system. The order of volatility requires collecting the most volatile data first. Which data source is the MOST volatile and must be collected first?",options:["Contents of the local hard drive","Remote network shares","CPU registers and CPU cache","Archived backup tapes"],correct:2,explanation:"The order of volatility (most to least): CPU registers and cache → RAM → Swap/page file → Local disk → Remote network storage → Archival/backup media. CPU registers contain the currently executing instructions and operand data that is lost the instant the CPU state changes or loses power. This data must be captured first while the system remains running.",wrongReasons:["Local hard drive contents are non-volatile and persist after power loss; they should be forensically imaged after more volatile data is collected.","Remote network shares are the least volatile of the live data sources listed; they persist on remote servers and can be collected after local volatile data.","null","Archival backup tapes retain data for years without power; they are the least volatile storage medium and are collected last in the order of volatility."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 4.6 — Incident Response",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 4.6 — Incident Response",messerSection:"4.6",difficulty:"hard"},
{id:"c2-4.6-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"4.0",domainName:"Operational Procedures",objective:"4.6",objectiveText:"Explain the processes for incident response.",question:"A chain of custody document is maintained throughout a forensic investigation. What is the PRIMARY purpose of chain of custody documentation?",options:["To create backup copies of forensic disk images","To ensure evidence integrity and legal admissibility by tracking who handled evidence and when","To speed up the forensic investigation process","To allow multiple analysts to work on the same original device simultaneously"],correct:1,explanation:"Chain of custody documents every individual who accessed, collected, transferred, analyzed, or stored evidence — along with the date, time, and purpose. This creates an unbroken record proving evidence was not tampered with from collection through court presentation. Without proper chain of custody, evidence may be challenged as tainted and deemed inadmissible.",wrongReasons:["Creating forensic image copies is a separate technical task performed by the analyst; chain of custody is a documentation and tracking process.","null","Chain of custody adds procedural rigor with required documentation; speed is not its purpose and proper custody procedures may add time.","Multiple analysts working on the same original evidence simultaneously would contaminate the evidence and break chain of custody; forensic copies (bit-for-bit images) are used for analysis instead."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 4.6 — Incident Response",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 4.6 — Incident Response",messerSection:"4.6",difficulty:"medium"},
{id:"c2-4.8-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"4.0",domainName:"Operational Procedures",objective:"4.8",objectiveText:"Identify the basics of scripting.",question:"A company purchases software that requires an annual payment renewal. If they stop paying, access is revoked. Which software license model does this describe?",options:["Perpetual license","Open-source license","Subscription license","Site license"],correct:2,explanation:"A subscription license requires ongoing periodic payments (monthly or annually) to maintain access. If the subscription lapses, the software deactivates or loses full functionality. Microsoft 365, Adobe Creative Cloud, and most modern SaaS tools use subscription licensing. This contrasts with perpetual licenses where a one-time payment grants indefinite use of that version.",wrongReasons:["A perpetual license grants permanent usage rights for the software version purchased after a single payment; no renewals are required to continue using the software.","An open-source license (GPL, MIT, Apache) grants rights to use, modify, and distribute software freely under specific conditions; it is not a payment model.","null","A site license grants usage rights for a fixed location or unlimited users at an organization, typically as a perpetual or annual purchase; it describes the scope of licensing, not payment frequency."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 4.8 — Licensing",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 4.8 — Licensing",messerSection:"4.8",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-4.9-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"4.0",domainName:"Operational Procedures",objective:"4.9",objectiveText:"Given a scenario, use proper communication techniques and professionalism.",question:"A technician is repairing a user's laptop and notices personal financial documents and private photos visible on the desktop. What is the MOST professional action?",options:["Browse the files to better understand the user's computing habits","Screenshot sensitive documents in case they are evidence of wrongdoing","Focus only on the authorized repair task and avoid accessing or viewing any personal files","Delete the personal files to improve system organization"],correct:2,explanation:"Professional technicians must respect user privacy. Their authorization is limited to the specific repair task — not browsing, copying, or viewing personal data. Unauthorized access to personal files may violate privacy laws (like CFAA), professional codes of ethics, and company policies. Only access what is explicitly required to complete the assigned work.",wrongReasons:["Browsing personal files without authorization is a serious breach of professional ethics and privacy, even with good intentions.","Screenshotting personal documents without authorization constitutes unauthorized data collection and potentially illegal privacy violation.","null","Deleting user files without explicit permission — even under the guise of cleanup — could destroy irreplaceable personal data and constitutes unauthorized system modification."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 4.9 — Professionalism",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 4.9 — Professionalism",messerSection:"4.9",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-4.10-001",exam:"core2",domainNum:"4.0",domainName:"Operational Procedures",objective:"4.10",objectiveText:"Identify the basics of scripting.",question:"A Windows administrator creates a script that automates user account creation across multiple servers. The file uses the .ps1 extension. Which scripting environment does this file use?",options:["Python (.py extension)","Batch file (.bat extension)","PowerShell (.ps1 extension)","Bash (.sh extension)"],correct:2,explanation:"PowerShell scripts use the .ps1 file extension. PowerShell is Microsoft's task automation and configuration management framework built on .NET, providing cmdlets for managing Windows, Active Directory, Azure, and Microsoft 365. Execution of .ps1 scripts is controlled by the system's execution policy (Get-ExecutionPolicy / Set-ExecutionPolicy).",wrongReasons:["Python scripts use the .py extension; while Python runs on Windows, it is not the native Windows automation framework associated with .ps1 files.","Batch files use the .bat or .cmd extension and use legacy Command Prompt syntax; they are far less capable than PowerShell for modern Windows administration.","null","Bash scripts use the .sh extension and run primarily on Linux/macOS; while Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL) supports Bash, .sh files are not Windows PowerShell scripts."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 4.10 — Scripting",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 4.10 — Scripting",messerSection:"4.10",difficulty:"easy"},
{id:"c2-4.10-002",exam:"core2",domainNum:"4.0",domainName:"Operational Procedures",objective:"4.10",objectiveText:"Identify the basics of scripting.",question:"An AI assistant confidently states that a specific medication dosage is safe, but the information is incorrect — the AI generated it based on statistical patterns in training data rather than verified medical facts. What type of AI error is this?",options:["A zero-day vulnerability","An AI hallucination","A false positive detection","A logic bomb"],correct:1,explanation:"An AI hallucination occurs when a generative AI model produces confident-sounding but factually incorrect, fabricated, or unsupported content. This happens because AI models generate responses based on statistical patterns in training data rather than verified real-world knowledge. Critical verification from authoritative sources is essential, especially for high-stakes domains like medicine, law, and security.",wrongReasons:["A zero-day vulnerability is an unpatched software security flaw unknown to the vendor; it has no relation to AI generating incorrect factual content.","null","A false positive in security is when a benign item is incorrectly flagged as malicious (e.g., antivirus flagging a safe file); it is not the term for AI-generated inaccurate content.","A logic bomb is malicious code triggered by a specific condition or date; it has no relation to AI models generating incorrect responses."],source1:"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 Official Objectives, Domain 4.10 — AI Tools",source2:"Professor Messer A+ 220-1202, Section 4.10 — AI Tools",messerSection:"4.10",difficulty:"medium"}
